<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abdul Razak Hussein</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1922">1922</a>–<a href="/years/1976">1976</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Tun Abdul Razak</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abdul Razak Hussein, known as Tun Abdul Razak, was the second prime minister of Malaysia, from 1970 until his death in 1976. After the race riots of 13 May 1969 he headed the National Operations Council that governed during the emergency, then launched the New Economic Policy and formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barisan-nasional">Barisan Nasional</a> coalition. He established relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in 1974, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najib-razak">Najib Razak</a> later became prime minister.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abdul-razak-hussein.jpg" alt="Abdul Razak Hussein" width="320" height="416" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Malaysian Government · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tun_Abdul_Razak_Official_Portrait_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Abdul Razak was born on 11 March 1922 in Kampung Pulau Keladi, a village northwest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pekan">Pekan</a> in Pahang. He was the first of two children of Hussein Awang bin Mohd Taib and Fatimah binti Daud, and an aristocratic descendant who held the title Orang Kaya Indera Shahbandar. He studied at the Malay College Kuala Kangsar and joined the Malay Administrative Service in 1938. A scholarship took him to Raffles College in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> in 1940, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> ended his studies.</p><p>During the war he helped organise the Wataniah resistance movement in Pahang and, according to the account given of him, served as an informant inside the Japanese administration. In 1948 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, and in 1950 he qualified as a barrister at Lincoln's Inn in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After returning in 1950 Razak joined the Malayan Civil Service and became youth chief of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-malays-national-organisation">United Malays National Organisation</a>. In February 1955, at 33, he became Chief Minister of Pahang. He won a seat in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaya">Malaya</a>'s first general elections in July 1955 and was appointed Education Minister, and he was instrumental in drafting the Razak Report, which formed the basis of the Malayan education system. He was a key member of the mission to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in February 1956 that sought independence for Malaya. From 1957 to 1970 he was deputy prime minister, and he held the defence portfolio, later also national and rural development. In 1965 he took part in the negotiations for the independence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>After the riots of 13 May 1969, Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunku-abdul-rahman">Tunku Abdul Rahman</a> was criticised by Malay nationalists within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-malays-national-organisation">UMNO</a> for his accommodating attitude towards non-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malays">Malays</a>. During the state of emergency of 1969 to 1971 Razak led the National Operations Council, which ruled in place of the elected government, and the Tunku resigned in 1970. Some contest the official account of these events.</p><p>As prime minister Razak launched the New Economic Policy to reduce socioeconomic disparity between ethnic communities, and in 1974 he set up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barisan-nasional">Barisan Nasional</a> coalition. Malaysia joined the Non-Aligned Movement in 1970 and signed the ZOPFAN declaration in 1971, and relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> began with his state visit in 1974. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> on 14 January 1976 while seeking medical treatment, and is called the Father of Development.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abdul Razak Hussein” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The racial riots of 13 May 1969 in Kuala Lumpur, which officially killed 196 people, led to the suspension of parliament, rule by a National Operations Council under <b>Tun Abdul Razak</b>, the national ideology of Rukun Negara and the New Economic Policy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/malaysia">Malaysia · Into the Present</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Kampung Pulau Keladi, in what is now <a href="/countries/malaysia">Malaysia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=3.52,103.38&amp;year=1957" aria-label="Kampung Pulau Keladi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7419 2032 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Abdul Razak Hussein"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7869 2302h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7869 2302h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=3.52,103.38&amp;year=1957">What was here in 1957? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
